WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:02.150
<v Examiner>Four marks.

2
00:00:02.150 --> 00:00:06.189
<v Examiner>Differentiate between population health and personal health.

3
00:00:06.189 --> 00:00:11.842
<v Examiner>Then explain how demography and epidemiology help nurses plan care for a population.

4
00:00:12.292 --> 00:00:16.665
Population health looks at the health outcomes of a group of people,

5
00:00:16.665 --> 00:00:19.945
and how those outcomes are spread across the group.

6
00:00:19.945 --> 00:00:23.868
The group can be big, like children in New Zealand, or small,

7
00:00:23.868 --> 00:00:27.213
like people over sixty-five with C O P D in Manukau.

8
00:00:27.213 --> 00:00:29.464
Its actions aim at the whole group.

9
00:00:29.464 --> 00:00:34.094
Personal health is the health of one individual: their own risk factors,

10
00:00:34.094 --> 00:00:39.368
symptoms and circumstances, usually one-to-one care between patient and clinician.

11
00:00:39.368 --> 00:00:44.320
Public health is the set of approaches used to improve a population's health.

12
00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:48.758
Here that's run by the Public Health Agency, Te Pou Hauora Tūmatanui,

13
00:00:48.758 --> 00:00:51.395
working towards pae ora, healthy futures.

14
00:00:51.395 --> 00:00:55.253
Think safe drinking water, smokefree laws, and immunisation.

15
00:00:55.703 --> 00:01:01.387
Demography is the study of human populations: their size, distribution and structure.

16
00:01:01.387 --> 00:01:07.405
Structure means fertility, mortality, migration, marriage and family, and social mobility.

17
00:01:07.405 --> 00:01:10.213
Epidemiology is the study of the patterns,

18
00:01:10.213 --> 00:01:13.556
causes and control of disease in groups of people.

19
00:01:13.556 --> 00:01:19.106
Its studies are either observational, which watch what happens without stepping in,

20
00:01:19.106 --> 00:01:23.787
or experimental, which actively change something and see what happens.

21
00:01:24.237 --> 00:01:26.081
How they help a nurse plan.

22
00:01:26.081 --> 00:01:28.198
Demography shows who is coming.

23
00:01:28.198 --> 00:01:31.954
Lisa's example: census data shows an ageing population,

24
00:01:31.954 --> 00:01:35.642
which means more demand for aged care and its workers,

25
00:01:35.642 --> 00:01:39.329
more people with several long-term conditions at once,

26
00:01:39.329 --> 00:01:41.788
and the need for workforce planning.

27
00:01:41.788 --> 00:01:47.798
Epidemiology shows what's spreading and why, so nurses can screen, monitor risk factors,

28
00:01:47.798 --> 00:01:49.300
vaccinate and educate.

29
00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:50.529
Two terms to know.

30
00:01:50.529 --> 00:01:54.900
Endemic is the stable, expected level, like a normal flu season.

31
00:01:54.900 --> 00:01:58.315
Epidemic means cases in excess of what's expected.

32
00:01:58.765 --> 00:02:03.326
Population health: outcomes across a group, actions for the group.

33
00:02:03.326 --> 00:02:06.436
Personal health: one person, one-to-one care.

34
00:02:06.436 --> 00:02:11.965
Demography: size and structure of a population, fertility, mortality, migration.

35
00:02:11.965 --> 00:02:17.840
Epidemiology: patterns, causes and control of disease, observational or experimental.

36
00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:22.125
Endemic is the expected level; epidemic is more than expected.
